practiceProblems.solutionCode:
class Employee {
    String name;
    int id;

    Employee() {
        name = "Guest";
        id = 0;
    }

    Employee(String n, int i) {
        name = n;
        id = i;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Employee e1 = new Employee();
        Employee e2 = new Employee("Aditi", 101);
        System.out.println("E1: " + e1.name + " (ID: " + e1.id + ") | E2: " + e2.name + " (ID: " + e2.id + ")");
    }
}

class Product {
    String name;
    double price;

    Product() {
        name = "Generic";
        price = 0.0;
    }

    Product(String n) {
        name = n;
        price = 9.99;
    }

    Product(String n, double p) {
        name = n;
        price = p;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Product p1 = new Product();
        Product p2 = new Product("Pen");
        Product p3 = new Product("Book", 19.99);
        System.out.println("P1: " + p1.name + " ($" + p1.price + ") | P2: " + p2.name + " ($" + p2.price + ") | P3: " + p3.name + " ($" + p3.price + ")");
    }
}

class Identifier {
    String code;

    Identifier(int num) {
        code = "NUM-" + num;
    }

    Identifier(String str) {
        code = "STR-" + str;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Identifier id1 = new Identifier(404);
        Identifier id2 = new Identifier("ABC");
        System.out.println("ID1: " + id1.code + " | ID2: " + id2.code);
    }
}

practiceProblems.description:
A developer wrote two constructors trying to distinguish them by variable name:

```java
class User {
    String username;
    User(String email) { username = email; }
    User(String phone) { username = phone; } // ❌ Duplicate error!
}
```

Fix the design: Make constructor 2 take an `int phoneNumber` instead, setting `username = "PH-" + phoneNumber`.
In `Main`, create `u1 = new User("dev@java.com")` and `u2 = new User(98765)`.
Print: `User 1: dev@java.com | User 2: PH-98765`.

